# deploy/Caddyfile.example — a real TLS terminator in front of
# a2a_robot_server.lex's plain-HTTP A2A endpoint.
#
# Lex's std.net has no TLS option on the plain-HTTP serve path
# (net.serve_fn / net.serve_fn_with) — TLS in this toolchain is wired
# only to the QUIC/HTTP-3 transport (net.serve_quic*, backed by
# std.tls), which most A2A clients (curl, ADK, LangGraph, CrewAI,
# AutoGen...) don't speak, and which this project's pinned lex release
# isn't even built with (`--features quic` is off by default). See
# README.md's "Securing a public endpoint: session/open" section for
# the full reasoning.
#
# The standard fix, used here: terminate TLS at a reverse proxy and
# forward plain HTTP to the robot's A2A port on loopback. Caddy gets
# you automatic Let's Encrypt certs for a real domain with no extra
# config beyond this file.
#
# Usage:
#   1. Point a2a-robot.example.com's DNS A/AAAA record at this host.
#   2. Start the robot's A2A server as usual, e.g.:
#        lex run --allow-effects io,time,crypto,random,sql,fs_read,fs_write,net,concurrent,sense,actuate \
#          examples/a2a_robot_demo.lex run
#   3. caddy run --config deploy/Caddyfile.example --adapter caddyfile
#   4. Point clients at https://a2a-robot.example.com/ instead of
#      http://localhost:8766/ — everything else (session/open,
#      tasks/send, the AgentCard at /.well-known/agent.json) is
#      unchanged, since Caddy just forwards the request bytes.
#
# `require_https` in ConsentPolicy (src/a2a_consent.lex) checks a
# peer's own declared card endpoint, not the transport the incoming
# request arrived over — set it once your RobotCard's `endpoint` field
# is the https:// URL above, so a card that still advertises a plain
# http:// endpoint gets refused.
#
# Replace the domain below with your own, or swap in the local/dev
# block underneath for a self-signed cert with no public DNS needed.

a2a-robot.example.com {
    reverse_proxy localhost:8766
}

# --- local/dev alternative: self-signed cert, no public DNS needed ---
# localhost:8443 {
#     tls internal
#     reverse_proxy localhost:8766
# }
